    "output_text": "# Edit Distance (Levenshtein Distance) Using Dynamic Programming\n\n## Implementation\n\n```python\ndef edit_distance(s1: str, s2: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Compute the minimum number of single-character edits (insertions,\n    deletions, or substitutions) required to transform s1 into s2.\n\n    Args:\n        s1: The source string.\n        s2: The target string.\n\n    Returns:\n        The edit distance between s1 and s2.\n    \"\"\"\n    m, n = len(s1), len(s2)\n\n    # Create a (m+1) x (n+1) DP table\n    # dp[i][j] = edit distance between s1[:i] and s2[:j]\n    dp = [[0] * (n + 1) for _ in range(m + 1)]\n\n    # Base cases: transforming an empty string to s2[:j] requires j insertions\n    for j in range(n + 1):\n        dp[0][j] = j\n\n    # Base cases: transforming s1[:i] to an empty string requires i deletions\n    for i in range(m + 1):\n        dp[i][0] = i\n\n    # Fill the table using the recurrence relation\n    for i in range(1, m + 1):\n        for j in range(1, n + 1):\n            if s1[i - 1] == s2[j - 1]:\n                # Characters match: no operation needed\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1]\n            else:\n                # Take the minimum of three operations:\n                # 1. Deletion: dp[i-1][j] + 1\n                # 2. Insertion: dp[i][j-1] + 1\n                # 3. Substitution: dp[i-1][j-1] + 1\n                dp[i][j] = 1 + min(\n                    dp[i - 1][j],      # delete s1[i-1]\n                    dp[i][j - 1],      # insert s2[j-1]\n                    dp[i - 1][j - 1]   # substitute s1[i-1] with s2[j-1]\n                )\n\n    return dp[m][n]\n",
